=== start ===
# You meet the NPC at a bustling market, where people are discussing the government’s new economic initiative.

#speaker: Player  
Hey, have you heard about this new economic initiative? They're calling it a game-changer, but I think there’s more to it than they’re saying.

* [Think about it. How often do these initiatives actually help ordinary people?] -> logical_tone
* [It’s upsetting, isn’t it? They promise prosperity, but it’s always the big corporations that profit.] -> emotional_tone
* [Don’t you find it strange how quickly they pushed this through without public input?] -> argumentative_tone
* [I get it. It sounds good on the surface, but maybe we should take a closer look.] -> reserved_tone

=== logical_tone ===
#speaker: Player  
Think about it. How often do these initiatives actually help ordinary people? If you look at the track record, the benefits rarely trickle down.

#speaker: other  
I guess that’s true, but what if this time it’s different? Maybe they’ve learned from past mistakes.

* [History has shown us that these promises often come with strings attached. Why would this time be any different?] -> logical_tone
* [I hope so, but can we afford to take that risk when so many people are struggling?] -> emotional_tone
* [That’s exactly what they want you to think—to make you drop your guard.] -> argumentative_tone
* [It’s worth hoping, but let’s make sure we’re not being misled.] -> reserved_tone

=== emotional_tone ===
#speaker: Player  
It’s upsetting, isn’t it? They promise prosperity, but it’s always the big corporations that profit. How many times have we heard this story?

#speaker: other  
I know, but what choice do we have? If we don’t support it, we could miss out on opportunities.

* [But real opportunities don’t come at the expense of the people. We need sustainable solutions.] -> logical_tone
* [That’s the heartbreaking part—they make us feel like we have no choice, but we do.] -> emotional_tone
* [Is it really an opportunity if only a few benefit while the rest are left behind?] -> argumentative_tone
* [You’re right to be cautious. Let’s make sure we have all the facts first.] -> reserved_tone

=== argumentative_tone ===
#speaker: Player  
Don’t you find it strange how quickly they pushed this through without public input? It’s like they don’t want us to ask questions.

#speaker: other  
Maybe, but sometimes decisions have to be made quickly to keep the economy moving.

* [True, but transparency and accountability shouldn’t be sacrificed for speed.] -> logical_tone
* [But isn’t it alarming how decisions like these always seem to benefit the same groups?] -> emotional_tone
* [That’s the narrative they sell us. But real progress doesn’t happen in the shadows.] -> argumentative_tone
* [You have a point. Let’s just make sure we understand the full picture.] -> reserved_tone

=== reserved_tone ===
#speaker: Player  
I get it. It sounds good on the surface, but maybe we should take a closer look. Big promises often come with hidden costs.

#speaker: other  
You’re not wrong. It’s probably a good idea to dig a little deeper before jumping to conclusions.

* [Exactly. The truth often lies in the details they try to gloss over.] -> logical_tone
* [I just don’t want us to regret trusting something that could hurt us in the long run.] -> emotional_tone
* [We owe it to ourselves to question their motives. Let’s not take this at face value.] -> argumentative_tone
* [Take your time. I’ll be here if you want to discuss it further.] -> reserved_tone
